{
  "id": "8.2-ideal-gas",
  "ch": 8,
  "openstax_section": "OpenStax AF2e §8.2",
  "guide_line": "Use the gas laws and PV = nRT with kelvin temperature.",
  "steps": [
    "Every gas-law temperature must be in kelvin. Add 273.15 to a Celsius reading. 25.00 °C is 298.15 K.",
    "Boyle: if temperature and moles stay fixed, pressure and volume trade off. P1 V1 = P2 V2.",
    "Charles: if pressure and moles stay fixed, volume grows with kelvin temperature. V1/T1 = V2/T2.",
    "Amontons: if volume and moles stay fixed, pressure grows with kelvin temperature. P1/T1 = P2/T2. The same constant-volume relation is also called Gay-Lussac's law.",
    "Avogadro: if temperature and pressure stay fixed, volume grows with moles. V1/n1 = V2/n2.",
    "All of those sit inside PV = nRT. Use R = 0.08206 L atm/(mol K) when P is in atm. Use R = 8.314 L kPa/(mol K) when P is in kPa.",
    "STP in this book is 273.15 K and 1 atm (101.325 kPa). One mole of ideal gas occupies 22.41 L there."
  ],
  "worked_example": {
    "q": "What is the volume of 1.000 mol of ideal gas at STP? Use R = 0.08206 L atm/(mol K). STP is 1 atm and 273.15 K.",
    "steps": [
      "STP fixes P = 1 atm and T = 273.15 K.",
      "V = nRT/P = (1.000)(0.08206)(273.15)/1.000.",
      "V = 22.41 L."
    ],
    "answer": "22.41 L"
  },
  "similar_problem": {
    "q": "What is the volume of 0.5000 mol of ideal gas at STP? Use R = 0.08206 L atm/(mol K).",
    "hint": "At STP, multiply 0.5000 mol by 22.41 L/mol.",
    "answer": "11.21 L"
  },
  "sources": [
    "OpenStax AF2e §8.2"
  ],
